---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A head-up display symbology set drawn in gold on near black: a pitch ladder climbing away from the horizon line, a flight path marker, an angle-of-attack bracket, and airspeed and altitude tapes at either edge"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">This course does not teach air combat.</p>

  <p>
    It teaches you to take a single-seat jet off the ground, fly one circuit,
    and land it. Twelve weeks, and that is the whole syllabus. The hard part
    was never the shooting — it is that this aircraft is difficult to fly,
    expensive to break, and has nobody in it but you.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Four weeks on the ground, four in the simulator, and then you fly. Week 1
    teaches egress, because it is the skill with the shortest deadline. Weeks 2
    to 4 cover why this airframe cannot fly without the computer that flies it,
    what that computer will refuse to let you do, and the energy arithmetic
    every later week spends. Weeks 5 to 8 put you in the box until the circuit
    is boring.
  </p>
  <p>
    In week 10 you fly the aircraft. This type has no second seat, so your
    first flight in it is solo, and the nine weeks before it exist because of
    that. Week 12 is a check ride: one takeoff, one circuit, one landing.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Final-year students who have done a first course in aerodynamics and can
    manipulate an energy equation without looking it up. No flying experience
    is assumed. A tolerance for being told no by a machine is useful, and you
    will develop one regardless.
  </p>

  <h2>How you are graded</h2>
  <p>
    On one question, asked at the end of every sortie and worth half the
    course at the end of the last one: can this aircraft fly again tomorrow,
    and was that the result of what you did rather than what you got away with?
  </p>
  <p>
    Abandoning an approach costs you nothing here. Continuing past a gate you
    set yourself is the failure, and it stays one even when the landing turns
    out fine.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, and what happens in each one.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Standards" href="/standards/">
      <p>Every number you are held to, on one page.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Two gates, one written judgement, and the check ride.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this, and who owns the aircraft.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin-block-end: 1rem;
  }
</style>
